## Migration Analysis

Your code contains **three patterns** that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort` | `CancellationToken` |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | catch block with `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort to continue | Keeps thread alive after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` and continue |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue or exit the loop.
                // The while condition will exit on next iteration.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, you cannot force-kill it.
        // The thread MUST cooperate. Log a warning if it hasn't stopped.
        if (_workerThread?.IsAlive == true)
        {
            // Log: "Worker thread did not stop within timeout"
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

## Key Changes Explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — signals intent; doesn't force-kill.
2. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (...)`** — the `when` guard prevents catching unrelated OCEs.
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed** — cooperative cancellation doesn't rethrow; the loop naturally continues or exits.
4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(TimeSpan)`** — actually waits for thread exit instead of just hoping.
5. **`GetNextItem()` must accept `CancellationToken`** — this is critical; if it blocks on I/O or a queue, pass the token so it can unblock.

---

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| `GetNextItem()` ignores the token | If it blocks indefinitely (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take()`), cancellation stalls. Use `Take(token)` or async alternatives. |
| No `Dispose` on `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks internal timers/callbacks. Always dispose. |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without `when` guard | May swallow cancellations from unrelated sources. |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem` runs for 30s without checking, you wait 30s. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long work. |
| No fallback for truly uncooperative code | If `GetNextItem` calls native/third-party code you can't modify, move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`. There is no in-process alternative to `Thread.Abort` in .NET 8. |

---

## If `GetNextItem` uses a BlockingCollection

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
private object GetNextItem() => _queue.Take(); // blocks forever

// After (.NET 8)
private object GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// Throws OperationCanceledException when token fires — unblocks immediately.
```

This is the most common source of "cancellation doesn't work" bugs in this migration.
